= Occam's razor =

== Overview ==

{{:Occam's razor/Simple}}

'''Occam's razor''' denotes several related principles of parsimony in explanation, ontology and theory selection. The common recommendation is to avoid unnecessary commitments while taking evidence seriously. It cannot establish that a simpler model is true, that fewer numerical parameters always means better generalization, or that a historically attributed slogan is an exact medieval quotation.

== Types of simplicity ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Interpretation !! What is minimized or discouraged !! Key qualification
|-
| Ontological parsimony || Unnecessary kinds of entities or theoretical commitments || Competing theories may disagree about what counts as an entity
|-
| Syntactic or descriptive simplicity || Unneeded length or formal complexity of a representation || Coding conventions and descriptions may disguise assumptions
|-
| Parametric complexity || Additional tunable degrees of freedom || Number of parameters alone does not predict out-of-sample performance
|-
| Methodological economy || Redundant hypotheses or investigative steps || More complex evidence-driven hypotheses must remain admissible
|}

The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy distinguishes ontological simplicity from several forms of theoretical elegance and discusses competing justifications of the razor. The Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy documents its historically complicated association with William of Ockham, including earlier expressions of related principles.<ref name="simp_core">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/simplicity/ ''Simplicity''], historical and contemporary formulations of simplicity principles.</ref><ref name="hist_core">Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, [https://iep.utm.edu/ockham/ ''William of Ockham'']; Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ockham/ ''William of Ockham''], historical context and the difference between a modern label and medieval arguments.</ref>

== Scientific and mathematical uses ==

Some forms of statistical model selection trade fit against a specified form of complexity. Information criteria, Bayesian marginal evidence and minimum-description-length approaches implement distinct mathematical assumptions, not one shared proof of Occam's razor. Jorma Rissanen's 1978 work develops one original formal description-length approach.<ref name="rissanen_core">Jorma Rissanen, [https://doi.org/10.1016/0005-1098(78)90005-5 “Modeling by Shortest Data Description”], ''Automatica'' 14(5), 465–471 (1978).</ref>

A useful application must compare models on meaningful evidence and predictions, disclose complexity measures and evaluate the effects of omitted mechanisms. An especially simple explanation that contradicts observations should not survive merely because it is easy to state.

The subject relates to [[Related to::Epistemology|epistemology]], [[Related to::Scientific model|scientific modelling]], [[Related to::Reasoning|reasoning]] and [[Related to::Abstraction|abstraction]]. A shorter description is not necessarily an abstraction that preserves the correct observables.

